class Solution {
  public int pairedDrawersFloorTicket(int[][] drawers, int k) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int i=0,j=0;while(i<a[0].length||j<a[1].length){if(j==a[1].length||(i<a[0].length&&a[0][i]<=a[1][j]))out.add(a[0][i++]);else out.add(a[1][j++]);}}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.min(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

drawers contains two lists, each already sorted from smallest to largest. Combine their numbers into one sorted result list. Keep all repeated numbers, including copies from both input lists.

Return the answer

Return the smallest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the combined length of the two lists. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

